<section
  id='case-rainydays-design'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Rainy Days — Web &amp; Interaction Design | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='Responsive e-commerce UX/UI case study focused on product discovery, clear calls to action, checkout flow, and reusable interface patterns.'
  data-case='rainydays'
  aria-labelledby='case-rainydays-design-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web &amp; Interaction Design</p>
        <h1 id='case-rainydays-design-title'>Rainy Days</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A responsive online shop designed to guide customers from finding a jacket to completing
          their purchase.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI design and prototyping</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual course project</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Focus</dt>
            <dd>E-commerce journey</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Figma, responsive UI, prototyping, components</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Complete shopping flow</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='rainydays-design'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Rainy Days sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='rainydays'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Rainy Days was created as an online shop for outdoor jackets. Customers needed to be able to
        browse the collection, view product information, add a jacket to the cart, and complete the
        checkout process. The design also needed to feel suitable for an outdoor brand while keeping
        the products and shopping actions easy to find.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            An online shop uses many of the same elements across different screens. If cards,
            buttons, prices, or form fields behave differently, the shopping experience can quickly
            become confusing. The main challenge was to keep the journey consistent from the product
            listing through to the order confirmation, including on smaller screens.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Help customers compare products without overcrowding the cards
            </li>
            <li>
              Keep the next step clear throughout the purchase process
            </li>
            <li>
              Adapt the product grid, forms, and checkout layout for mobile use
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-rainydays-600.webp'
            data-src='web-rainydays-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='515'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Rainy Days mobile designs showing product discovery, details, cart, checkout, and confirmation screens'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Mobile screens covering the complete shopping journey
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I kept the product cards consistent so customers could quickly compare images, names,
              and prices while browsing.
            </li>
            <li>
              Product images and purchasing information receive the most attention. Colour and
              decorative details are used more carefully so they do not compete with the jackets.
            </li>
            <li>
              Primary actions such as adding a product to the cart and continuing to checkout are
              visually stronger than secondary navigation.
            </li>
            <li>
              On smaller screens, I simplified the layouts and added more space around buttons and
              form controls to make them easier to use by touch.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-rainydays-desktop-600.webp'
            data-src='web-rainydays-desktop-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='626'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Rainy Days desktop product listing design with jacket cards, prices, and calls to action'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Desktop product grid with consistent cards and clear purchasing information
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block'>
          <h2>Purchase flow</h2>
          <p>
            I connected the high-fidelity screens into one prototype so I could check the whole
            journey rather than designing each page separately. This made it easier to see whether
            customers always knew what had been added to the cart, what they needed to do next, and
            whether their order had been completed.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Mapped the journey from browsing to product details, cart, checkout, and confirmation
            </li>
            <li>
              Planned feedback for form validation and an empty cart
            </li>
            <li>
              Kept the order details visible so customers could check their choices before
              completing the purchase
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-rainydays-flow-600.webp'
            data-src='web-rainydays-flow-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='346'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Rainy Days responsive e-commerce screens showing product details, checkout, and confirmation'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            High-fidelity flow from viewing a product to receiving order confirmation
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The final design covers the complete shopping journey on desktop and mobile. This was one of
        my earlier course projects, and it helped me understand how much consistency matters in an
        online shop. Reusing the same card, button, and form patterns made the different pages feel
        connected and made the purchase process easier to follow. In later projects, I have
        developed this further by paying more attention to accessibility, feedback states, and
        content that can change after the design is finished.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>